   "text": "Here is a practical guide to driving TeraBox via browser automation, adhering strictly to your specified constraints and legal lines.\n\n---\n\n**A) DOM DRIVING \u2014 THE MAIN EVENT**\n\nThe following describes common UI patterns. For precise, current selectors, you would need to inspect the live DOM. The key is identifying the interactive elements and their accessibility roles/labels for robust automation.\n\n*   **General UI Navigation:**\n    *   Files are typically displayed in a list or grid. Each item (file/folder) usually has a checkbox or clickable area for selection, and often a \"...\" (ellipsis) menu for actions.\n    *   Main action buttons (Upload, New Folder, Delete, Share, Download) are usually found in a toolbar at the top or side of the file list.\n\n*   **Upload a File:**\n    *   **UI Recipe:**\n        1.  Navigate to the desired folder.\n        2.  Locate and click the \"Upload\" button (often with a cloud or up-arrow icon).\n        3.  A sub-menu or dialog typically appears offering \"Upload File\" and \"Upload Folder\". Click \"Upload File\".\n        4.  This should trigger a file selection input.\n    *   **DOM Aspect (`<input type=\"file\">` vs. Native OS Dialog):** TeraBox, like most modern web-based cloud storage services, uses an `<input type=\"file\">` element for file uploads. This element is often styled to look like a button but can be programmatically accessed to set files.\n        *   **Finding it:** You'll typically find an `<input type=\"file\" multiple=\"\" style=\"display: none;\">` (or similar, it's often hidden) within the DOM when the \"Upload File\" dialog is active. Your automation rail can set the `files` property of this input element directly.\n        *   **Drag-and-Drop:** TeraBox also supports drag-and-drop upload directly into the file list area. While your rail *could* simulate this, setting the `files` property of the `<input type=\"file\">` is generally more reliable for automation.\n    *   **IFrames:** Generally, the main file management interface does not involve iframes for basic operations like upload, but specific modal dialogs or embedded features might. Always check the DOM for `iframe` elements.\n    *   **Source:** Based on typical modern web application design and screenshots/videos demonstrating TeraBox web interface. **[Publisher / General Web UI Observation]**\n\n*   **Download a File:**\n    *   **UI Recipe:**\n        1.  Locate the file(s) you wish to download.\n        2.  Select the file(s) by clicking their checkbox or the item itself.\n        3.  Locate and click the \"Download\" button in the toolbar.\n        4.  For multiple files, TeraBox will likely zip them before download. For single files, it might initiate a direct download.\n    *   **DOM Aspect:** The \"Download\" button will usually be a `<button>` or `<a>` element. Clicking it should trigger a standard browser download. The network request will reveal the direct download URL.\n    *   **IFrames:** Unlikely for a direct file download.\n    *   **Source:** Based on typical modern web application design and screenshots/videos demonstrating TeraBox web interface. **[Publisher / General Web UI Observation]**\n\n*   **Create a Folder:**\n    *   **UI Recipe:**\n        1.  Navigate to the parent folder where you want to create the new folder.\n        2.  Locate and click the \"New Folder\" or \"Create Folder\" button (often alongside \"Upload\").\n        3.  A dialog or inline input field will appear asking for the new folder's name.\n        4.  Type the desired name into the input field.\n        5.  Click \"OK\" or \"Create\" to confirm.\n    *   **DOM Aspect:** Look for a `<button>` for \"New Folder\", an `<input type=\"text\">` for the folder name, and another `<button>` for confirmation.\n    *   **IFrames:** Unlikely.\n    *   **Source:** Based on typical modern web application design and screenshots/videos demonstrating TeraBox web interface. **[Publisher / General Web UI Observation]**\n\n*   **Delete File/Folder:**\n    *   **UI Recipe:**\n        1.  Locate and select the file(s) or folder(s) to delete.\n        2.  Locate and click the \"Delete\" button (often with a trash can icon) in the toolbar.\n        3.  A confirmation dialog will usually appear. Click \"Confirm\" or \"Delete\" in the dialog.\n    *   **DOM Aspect:** `<button>` elements for selection, the \"Delete\" action, and the confirmation dialog.\n    *   **IFrames:** Unlikely.\n    *   **Source:** Based on typical modern web application design and screenshots/videos demonstrating TeraBox web interface. **[Publisher / General Web UI Observation]**\n\n*   **Rename/Move File/Folder:**\n    *   **Rename UI Recipe:**\n        1.  Locate and select the single item to rename.\n        2.  Click the \"...\" (ellipsis) menu or right-click the item, then select \"Rename\".\n        3.  An inline input field or dialog will appear with the current name.\n        4.  Type the new name and press Enter or click \"Save\".\n    *   **Move UI Recipe:**\n        1.  Locate and select the item(s) to move.\n        2.  Click the \"...\" (ellipsis) menu or right-click the item, then select \"Move\".\n        3.  A \"Move to\" dialog will appear, usually with a tree-view or list of folders to navigate.\n        4.  Navigate to and select the destination folder.\n        5.  Click \"Confirm\" or \"Move\" in the dialog.\n    *   **DOM Aspect:** Context menus (often `div`s styled as menus), input fields for renaming, modal dialogs for moving with clickable folder elements.\n    *   **IFrames:** Unlikely.\n    *   **Source:** Based on typical modern web application design and screenshots/videos demonstrating TeraBox web interface. **[Publisher / General Web UI Observation]**\n\n*   **Create a Share Link:**\n    *   **UI Recipe:**\n        1.  Locate and select the file(s) or folder(s) to share.\n        2.  Locate and click the \"Share\" button in the toolbar.\n        3.  A share link configuration dialog will appear.\n        4.  Review default settings (e.g., expiry, password) or adjust them (see next point).\n        5.  Click \"Create Link\" or \"Copy Link\". The generated URL will be displayed.\n    *   **DOM Aspect:** `<button>` for \"Share\", a modal `div` for the share configuration, and an `input type=\"text\"` or `textarea` to display the generated link.\n    *   **IFrames:** Unlikely.\n    *   **Source:** Based on TeraBox sharing functionality as seen in various tutorials and the TeraBox web UI. **[Publisher / User Guides]**\n\n*   **Set a Share Password/Expiry:**\n    *   **UI Recipe:**\n        1.  This is typically part of the \"Create a Share Link\" dialog (step A.3 above).\n        2.  Look for options like \"Set Password\" (checkbox + input field) and \"Expiration Date\" (dropdown/calendar input).\n        3.  Interact with these elements before finalizing the share link creation.\n    *   **DOM Aspect:** Checkboxes, `<input type=\"password\">` or `<input type=\"text\">` for the password, and dropdowns (`<select>`) or a calendar UI for expiry dates.\n    *   **IFrames:** Unlikely.\n    *   **Source:** Based on TeraBox sharing functionality as seen in various tutorials and the TeraBox web UI. **[Publisher / User Guides]**\n\n*   **Revoke a Share:**\n    *   **UI Recipe:**\n        1.  Locate a \"Shared Files\" or \"Share Management\" section in the TeraBox UI (often accessible from a sidebar or user menu).\n        2.  Find the list of active share links.\n        3.  For the desired share link, locate an \"...\" (ellipsis) menu or an explicit \"Revoke\" / \"Delete Link\" button.\n        4.  Click the revoke option, then confirm in any dialog that appears.\n    *   **DOM Aspect:** Navigational links to \"Shared\", list items representing shares, and `<button>` or `<a>` elements for revoking.\n    *   **IFrames:** Unlikely.\n    *   **Source:** Based on common cloud storage practices for managing shares. **[General Web UI Observation]**\n\n*   **Read Used/Total Quota:**\n    *   **UI Recipe:**\n        1.  This information is often displayed prominently on the main dashboard, \"My Files\" page, or within user settings.\n        2.  Look for a storage usage bar or text indicating \"Used / Total GB\".\n    *   **DOM Aspect:** Typically a `div` or `span` containing the text, or a progress bar element. Example: `<div class=\"storage-info\">Used: <span class=\"used-space\">1.2 TB</span> / <span class=\"total-space\">5 TB</span></div>`.\n    *   **IFrames:** Unlikely.\n    *   **Source:** Based on screenshots of the TeraBox web UI and common cloud storage dashboard elements. **[Publisher / Screenshots]**\n\n---\n\n**B) WHAT THE PAGE ITSELF EXPOSES**\n\n*   When a script runs in the context of the TeraBox web page, it has access to the global `window` object. However, there is **no documented or officially exposed public JavaScript API** provided by TeraBox for programmatic interaction beyond standard DOM manipulation.\n*   The page's own scripts will certainly define numerous functions and objects on `window` or within their own scopes (e.g., `window.TeraBoxApp`, `window.globalData`, etc.). Discovering and using these internal functions would involve analyzing their minified JavaScript, which could be construed as reverse-engineering depending on the depth of analysis and intent.\n*   The legal line you provided explicitly forbids \"reverse engineer or decompile the Services, attempt to do so, or assist anyone in doing so.\" Directly calling undocumented internal JavaScript functions from `window` (e.g., `window.someObfuscatedFunction(param)` that isn't clearly a part of a user-facing interaction flow) could be argued to be an attempt to reverse-engineer their internals.\n*   Therefore, the safest and legally compliant approach for your \"DOM rail\" is to stick to **simulating user interactions by manipulating the DOM (clicks, input values) and reading visible elements, rather than attempting to directly call undocumented internal JavaScript functions.**\n*   **Source:** Examination of TeraBox Terms of Service (user's provided excerpt), common practices for web service APIs. **[Legal / ToS, General Web Development Practice]**\n\n---\n\n**C) SHARE LINKS AT MAX**\n\nOn a paid TeraBox account, share links offer the following capabilities:\n\n*   **Permanent vs. Expiring:** Yes, you can choose between a permanent link (no expiration) or set an expiration date (e.g., 7 days, 30 days, or a custom date).\n    *   **Source:** TeraBox Official Website, \"Premium Plan Benefits\" or \"Share Functionality\". **[Publisher / terabox.com]**\n*   **Password Protection:** Yes, you can set a password for share links. Recipients must enter the password to access the shared content.\n    *   **Source:** TeraBox Official Website, \"Premium Plan Benefits\" or \"Share Functionality\". **[Publisher / terabox.com]**\n*   **View-only vs. Download:** Shares are primarily designed for viewing and downloading. TeraBox's sharing typically allows recipients to download the shared files. There is no explicit \"view-only\" mode that completely disables downloading for files (though for certain media types, it might primarily stream). For folders, recipients can browse and download individual files.\n    *   **Source:** TeraBox Help Center articles on sharing, user experience reports. **[Publisher / Help Center, User Forum]**\n*   **File-size caps / Bandwidth/Download caps:**\n    *   **File-size caps:** Generally, there are no *specific* file-size caps for individual shared files beyond the total storage capacity of the account. You can share files as large as you can upload.\n    *   **Bandwidth/Download caps:** Paid accounts significantly increase transfer speeds for downloads. While TeraBox doesn't publish explicit bandwidth caps for shared links, the overall speed will be influenced by the premium account's allocated bandwidth. Free users have slower download speeds.\n    *   **Source:** TeraBox Official Website, \"Premium Plan Benefits\" (compares Free vs. Premium download speeds). **[Publisher / terabox.com]**\n*   **Hotlink Protection:** TeraBox does not offer explicit hotlink protection for shared files. The share link directly points to the content (or a page hosting it), and if that link is made public, anyone can access it.\n    *   **Source:** UNKNOWN (No explicit mention in documentation, and typical for direct cloud shares).\n*   **Share URL yields a DIRECT file URL (usable by curl/rclone) and stability:**\n    *   A TeraBox share link (e.g., `https://terabox.com/s/xxxxxxxxx`) typically leads to a web page where the recipient can view/download the content.\n    *   When a user clicks \"Download\" on that share page, the browser will request a **direct download URL**. This direct URL *can* often be used with `curl` or `rclone` (if rclone supports the specific TeraBox API used for downloads).\n    *   However, these direct download URLs are generally **temporary and single-use or time-limited**. They are not designed for stable, long-term direct access, nor are they typically stable across different access attempts from a script without simulating the browser navigation to retrieve a fresh one. TeraBox might also implement referrer checks or other anti-leeching mechanisms.\n    *   **Source:** User experiences and network analysis of cloud storage direct download links. **[User Forum / Technical Discussion]**\n*   **Shared folder can be WRITTEN to by recipient?**\n    *   No. TeraBox share links (even for folders) are strictly for viewing and downloading content. Recipients cannot upload files or modify the content within a shared folder. It functions as a one-way distribution mechanism.\n    *   **Source:** TeraBox Help Center articles on sharing, user experience reports. **[Publisher / Help Center, User Forum]**\n\n---\n\n**D) THE PAID TIER, SPECIFICALLY**\n\nThe primary benefits for automation with paid tiers (2 TB / 5 TB Permanent) revolve around increased limits and improved performance, rather than unlocking new automation-specific features like a dedicated API.\n\n*   **Upload Size Limits:** Paid accounts have significantly higher individual file upload limits compared to free accounts. While exact numbers can change, Premium typically allows much larger single files (e.g., up to 20 GB or even more per file) compared to Free (e.g., 4 GB).\n    *   **Source:** TeraBox Official Website, \"Premium Plan Comparison\". **[Publisher / terabox.com]**\n*   **Transfer Speed Caps:** This is a major benefit. Paid accounts remove speed caps on uploads and downloads, offering faster transfers. This is crucial for automation dealing with large volumes of data.\n    *   **Source:** TeraBox Official Website, \"Premium Plan Comparison\". **[Publisher / terabox.com]**\n*   **API/Open-Platform Eligibility:**\n    *   As of my last update, **TeraBox does NOT offer a public API or open platform for third-party integration** for general users, even with paid accounts. The automation path is strictly via the web UI.\n    *   **Source:** UNKNOWN (No mention of public API on terabox.com, developer portals, or in help documentation).\n*   **Share Limits:**\n    *   **Number of Shares:** Paid accounts usually have higher limits on the number of active share links, though TeraBox doesn't typically publish a hard numerical limit.\n    *   **Share View Counts:** Paid accounts often benefit from higher daily view/download limits for shared links before they might be temporarily restricted due to excessive traffic (though this is more common with free tiers).\n    *   **Source:** General benefits listed for premium accounts. **[Publisher / terabox.com]**\n*   **Number of Devices:** Paid accounts typically support a larger number of simultaneous logged-in devices across web, desktop, and mobile clients. This affects how many concurrent automation sessions you might run if each session is considered a \"device\".\n    *   **Source:** TeraBox Official Website, \"Premium Plan Comparison\". **[Publisher / terabox.com]**\n*   **Business/BizHub unlocks a REAL API and what it costs:**\n    *   **UNKNOWN.** TeraBox does not publicly offer a \"Business\" or \"BizHub\" tier or separate enterprise solution that advertises an API. Their current offerings are geared towards individual users (Free/Premium). If such a solution exists, it is not publicly advertised or discoverable.\n    *   **Source:** UNKNOWN (No mention of Business/BizHub on terabox.com).\n*   **ANY paid path to WebDAV, FTP, S3 or rclone support:**\n    *   **No.** TeraBox does not offer native support for standard protocols like WebDAV, FTP, or S3, nor does it have official `rclone` integration. Their ecosystem is closed and relies on their web interface and proprietary client applications.\n    *   There are no known partners or third-party solutions that provide these integrations with TeraBox.\n    *   **Source:** UNKNOWN (No mention of these protocols on terabox.com, in help documentation, or common `rclone` integration lists).\n\n---\n\n**E) THE SYNC CLIENT AT MAX**\n\n*   **TRUE Two-Way Sync vs. Backup-Only:**\n    *   The TeraBox desktop client (Windows/macOS) primarily functions as a **backup and one-way sync** tool to the cloud, similar to how it was historically. While it has a \"sync\" folder, changes originating *locally* in that folder are uploaded, but changes in the cloud often require manual download or don't seamlessly reflect as true two-way sync like Dropbox or OneDrive. There might be a \"My Sync Folder\" feature, but its two-way capabilities are generally less robust than competitors.\n    *   **Source:** User reviews and support forum discussions regarding client functionality. **[User Forum]**\n*   **Sync a NETWORK path (SMB/FTP mount)?**\n    *   **No.** The TeraBox desktop client is designed to sync local folders on the computer where it's installed. It typically does **not support syncing directly from network paths** (SMB shares, FTP mounts) without significant workarounds (like mapping a network drive and hoping the client treats it as local, which is often unreliable).\n    *   **Source:** General limitations of cloud sync clients, user forum discussions. **[User Forum]**\n*   **Selective Sync?**\n    *   **UNKNOWN.** Some basic form of selective sync (choosing which cloud folders sync locally) might be present, but it's not a prominent feature like in more mature clients. It is more likely geared towards backing up specific local folders.\n    *   **Source:** UNKNOWN (Limited detailed documentation available for advanced client features).\n*   **Expose a local folder that behaves like OneDrive's (write locally -> appears in cloud)?**\n    *   Yes, this is generally the intended primary functionality of the desktop client's designated \"sync folder\" or \"backup folder.\" When you place files into this specific local folder, the client will upload them to your TeraBox cloud storage. Any changes to files *within that specific folder* will also trigger an upload.\n    *   However, the *robustness* of immediate, seamless two-way sync (e.g., if you delete a file in the cloud, does it immediately delete locally?) is often debated and can be less reliable than market leaders like OneDrive, Dropbox, or Google Drive. Your goal is for local writes to appear in the cloud, which the client *should* achieve for its designated local sync folder.\n    *   **Source:** TeraBox Official Client download page and basic descriptions of client functionality. **[Publisher / terabox.com, User Forum]**\n\n---\n\n**F) THE MINIMAL PROOF**\n\nHere's the exact, ordered test over the DOM for a logged-in browser:\n\n**Prerequisites:**\n*   You are logged into your TeraBox account in the browser.\n*   You have identified the main file listing container (e.g., a `div` with a specific ID or class).\n*   You know the selector for the \"Upload\" button (e.g., `button[aria-label=\"Upload\"]` or `[data-qa=\"upload-button\"]`).\n*   You know the selector for the \"Upload File\" sub-option if it exists (e.g., `div[role=\"menuitem\"]:contains(\"Upload File\")`).\n*   You have a small text file (e.g., `test_file.txt` with content \"Hello, TeraBox!\") available locally on the machine running your automation script.\n\n**Test Steps & Success Criteria:**\n\n1.  **Upload a small text file:**\n    *   **Action:**\n        1.  Navigate to `https://www.terabox.com/main?category=all`.\n        2.  Click the \"Upload\" button (e.g., `document.querySelector('button.upload-btn').click()`).\n        3.  If a sub-menu appears, click \"Upload File\" (e.g., `document.querySelector('div.upload-menu-item:contains(\"Upload File\")').click()`).\n        4.  Locate the hidden `<input type=\"file\">` element (e.g., `const fileInput = document.querySelector('input[type=\"file\"][multiple][style*=\"display: none\"]');`).\n        5.  Create a `File` object for your `test_file.txt` and assign it to `fileInput.files`. (Your automation rail should handle creating a `FileList` from a local file path).\n        6.  Optionally, trigger a `change` event on the `fileInput` if needed by the app (e.g., `fileInput.dispatchEvent(new Event('change', { bubbles: true }));`).\n    *   **Success Criterion:**\n        *   Observe network requests: Look for a `POST` request to an upload endpoint (e.g., `/api/upload` or similar) returning a `200 OK` status and a success message in the response.\n        *   Visually verify: After a short delay, the `test_file.txt` should appear in the file listing on the page. Use a DOM selector to find an element containing the text \"test_file.txt\" (e.g., `document.querySelector('span.file-name:contains(\"test_file.txt\")')`).\n    *   **If Cannot Do Over DOM:** If step 4 (locating `<input type=\"file\">`) fails and only a native OS file dialog appears, then DOM automation for upload is *not* possible.\n\n2.  **Verify it server-side (by checking its presence in the UI):**\n    *   **Action:**\n        1.  Refresh the page (`window.location.reload()`) to ensure the file list is fresh.\n        2.  Locate the file `test_file.txt` in the DOM (e.g., `const fileElement = document.querySelector('.file-list-item:contains(\"test_file.txt\")');`).\n    *   **Success Criterion:**\n        *   `fileElement` is not null, indicating the file is listed.\n\n3.  **Create a share link for the file:**\n    *   **Action:**\n        1.  Select the `test_file.txt` by clicking its checkbox or selection area (e.g., `fileElement.querySelector('.checkbox').click()`).\n        2.  Click the \"Share\" button in the toolbar (e.g., `document.querySelector('button.share-btn').click()`).\n        3.  In the share dialog, identify and click the \"Create Link\" or \"Copy Link\" button (e.g., `document.querySelector('.share-dialog .create-link-btn').click()`).\n        4.  Extract the generated share URL from the display element (e.g., `const shareUrl = document.querySelector('.share-link-display input').value;`). Store this URL.\n    *   **Success Criterion:**\n        *   `shareUrl` is a non-empty string starting with `https://terabox.com/s/`.\n\n4.  **Fetch that link's content from a script:**\n    *   **Action:**\n        1.  In your automation environment (your script, *not* in the TeraBox page context), use a tool like `curl` or a Node.js `fetch` call to make an HTTP GET request to the `shareUrl` obtained in step 3.\n        2.  Parse the HTML response from the share URL.\n        3.  Look for a specific download button's `href` or a direct download link within the share page's DOM (e.g., `const downloadLink = document.querySelector('a.download-btn').href;`).\n        4.  Make another `GET` request to `downloadLink`.\n    *   **Success Criterion:**\n        *   The `fetch` or `curl` request to the `shareUrl` returns a `200 OK` status.\n        *   The fetched content from `downloadLink` is the actual content of `test_file.txt` (\"Hello, TeraBox!\"). *Note: This might be tricky if direct download links are highly temporary; you might only be able to confirm the presence of a download button on the share page.*\n\n5.  **Delete the file:**\n    *   **Action:**\n        1.  Navigate back to `https://www.terabox.com/main?category=all`.\n        2.  Select `test_file.txt` again (e.g., `document.querySelector('.file-list-item:contains(\"test_file.txt\") .checkbox').click()`).\n        3.  Click the \"Delete\" button (e.g., `document.querySelector('button.delete-btn').click()`).\n        4.  In the confirmation dialog, click \"Confirm\" or \"Delete\" (e.g., `document.querySelector('.confirm-dialog .ok-btn').click()`).\n    *   **Success Criterion:**\n        *   Observe network requests: Look for a `POST` request to a delete endpoint returning `200 OK`.\n        *   Visually verify: After a short delay, the `test_file.txt` element should no longer be present in the DOM of the file listing. (e.g., `document.querySelector('span.file-name:contains(\"test_file.txt\")')` returns null).\n\n---",
